** Turning builtin Led on

#+begin_src C
  # include <Arduino.h>
  void setup
  {
    pinMode(LEDBUILT, OUTPUT); // set the pin 13 to be in output
  }
  void loop ()
  {
    digitalWrite(LEDBUILTIN, HIGH); // set a low voltage
    delay(1000); // delay 1 sec
    digitalWrite(LEDBUILTIN, LOW); // send a low voltage
    delay(1000); // delay 1 sec
    // Code that will excuated while the Arduino is running
  }
#+end_src

* Circut to light a LED on

A red LED can turn with 1.2v and 5 to 20mA of current, but the Arduino
works with a current of 5v and very high current. To prevent the
led from recieving damage we would add a resistant to it.

** Formula for this:

\[
R = \frac{V_s - V_1}{I}
\]

The elements on this formula are
1. \(V_s\) is the current is the suply voltage (5v)
2. \(V_1\) is the LED forward voltage drop (1.7v)
3. \(I\)   is the current required by the led (10mA) in Amp so, (0.001A)

Replacing the values on the formula we would find that

\begin{aligned}
R &= \frac{5\,\text{V} - 1.7\,\text{V}}{0.001\,\text{A}}\\
R &= \frac{3.3\,\text{V}}{0.001\,\text{A}}\\
R &= 330\,\Omega
\end{aligned}

The general formula to the this is:

\begin{center}
V = I * R
\end{center}
V for voltage
I for current
R for resistance


